==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[luster]] {{qualifier|US}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈlʌstə/|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|la|en}} ''[[lux]]'', light

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# [[shine|Shine]], [[sheen]] [[gleam]] or [[polish]].
#: ''The brass had a characteristic '''lustre''' that practically glowed when it was freshly cleaned.''
# By extension, [[interest]], [[attractiveness]], or [[splendour]].
#: ''After so many years in the same field, the job had lost its '''lustre'''.''
# [[refinement|Refinement]], [[polish]], or [[quality]].
#: ''He spoke with all the '''lustre''' a seasoned enthusiast should have.''

=====Antonyms=====
* {{l|en|dullness}}
* {{l|en|lacklustre}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|lustreware}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|en|lustrous}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-see|luster}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|lustr|es}}

# {{context|transitive|rare|poetic|lang=en}} To make [[lustrous]].
#* Lowell
#*: Flooded and '''lustred''' with her loosened gold.

===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{lena}} {{term|lustrum|lang=la}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A period of [[five]] [[year]]s; a [[lustrum]].
#* Bolingbroke
#*: Both of us have closed the tenth '''lustre'''.

{{Webster 1913}}

